  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 214 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nConfidence in the day-to-day specifics remains relatively low from \nSaturday onward, although we do expect temperatures to climb back to \nslightly above average through early next week (for the most part), \nfollowed by a cooling trend toward mid-week. Precipitation chances \nare also rather nebulous during this timeframe, with sporadic \nopportunities for showers between Saturday and Monday, followed by \nexpanding probabilities Tuesday onward as a cold front approaches. \n\nSaturday through Monday, zonal to slightly northwesterly upper level \nflow will persist, with a weakness in the upper ridge lingering just \nto our southeast. Humidity will persist locally, but very few \nlocal forcing mechanisms are depicted in model guidance. Further\nwest across the central plains, though, it does appear that a \npersistent low-level jet will drive the development of one or \nmore convective, with relatively low confidence in their exact \nplacement and trajectory. While the focus of this activity is \nexpected to remain to our west, remnants of these storms may \ngraze our far western areas at times, and the lingering humidity\nmay be enough for us to see a few locally developing showers at\ntimes, too. Again, timing and placement is very uncertain, but \nareas of central/western MO are favored with the highest \nprecipitation probabilities (~30%). Much more dry time is \nexpected overall, though, and we don't see substantial \nopportunity for strong/severe storms locally. Meanwhile, above \naverage temperatures are favored, along with high humidity, but \nnot likely enough of either to produce headline-worthy heat \nindex values on a large scale. A weak cold front also adds an \nelement of uncertainty to the temperature forecast Monday, \nparticularly north of I-70. \n\nFrom Tuesday onward, there are indications that a stronger shortwave \nwill begin to suppress the upper ridge across the central CONUS, \nalthough variance among ensembles and ensemble clusters remains very \nhigh. Both the timing and strength of this trough are very \nuncertain, with differences of as much as 24 hours evident among \nclusters, along with significant differences in strength. As such, \nthe timing of a cold front and its resulting impacts also remains \nuncertain, although a cooling trend does appear likely over the \nlatter half of the week. Given the above, temperature spreads remain \nhigh throughout this period, with 10-15 degree interquartile ranges \nTuesday onward, albeit with a slow downward trend. Meanwhile, \nprecipitation chances also gradually increase during this period, \nbut again with very high variance among ensemble members. In short, \nslowly cooling temperatures are most likely Tuesday onward, with \nincreasing chances for showers and thunderstorms overall.",
